# grep-delegation-arms.manifest — what CLAUDE.md ASSUMES about Claude Code's
# `grep` shell function, recorded so a `cc` bump cannot invalidate it silently.
# your-org/nexus-code#1234.
#
# THE EXPOSURE. Claude Code's shell snapshot installs `grep` as a shell FUNCTION
# that runs ugrep embedded in the `claude` executable — except that it opens
# with a delegation loop whose `case` arms route certain ARGUMENTS straight to
# `command grep` (the system GNU binary) instead. Two adjacent CLAUDE.md entries
# straddle that split, and each is accurate only on its own side of it:
#
#   GREP-BRE-DIALECT      needs its patterns to REACH ugrep, so that a bare
#                         `grep -c '^\+\+\+'` is LOUD (invalid syntax, rc 2)
#                         while `command grep` is the silent wrong answer.
#   DASH-PATTERN-OPTION   says Mode 1 (`grep -vF '---'`) is LOUD under GNU and
#                         SILENT under ugrep, and that a bare call here reaches
#                         GNU. It reaches GNU ONLY because `---`-leading
#                         arguments are DELEGATED.
#
# Nothing pinned that arm set. It is a property of the HARNESS BUILD, not of
# this repo, so a version bump can move it with no diff anywhere a suite looks.
# It has already produced one defect: `#1186`'s "measured identical under GNU
# grep 3.1 and ugrep 7.8.4" was true for Mode 2 and FALSE for Mode 1, because
# the two "independent" measurements were the same binary answering twice —
# the delegation arm made them one instrument. This repo's own rule, firing
# inside the diff that documents it: a cross-check that agrees with itself is
# not verification (`#946` F1, `#931`).
#
# WHAT IS ASSERTED, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE ARM SET. Recording this host's arm set
# and comparing it verbatim would be GREEN FOR THIS OPERATOR AND RED FOR EVERY
# OTHER ONE — a harness-build property masquerading as a repo invariant. So the
# ARMS rows below are a REVIEW RECORD, never an assertion, and the assertion is
# on the DEPENDENCY: for each documented remedy, does a representative argument
# still land on the side of the split that remedy assumes? That question has the
# same right answer on every host, and when it changes, the documentation really
# has been invalidated there.
#
# Columns are TAB-separated. Three record types:
#
#   ARMS       <cc-version>   <the case arm set, verbatim>
#              A REVIEW RECORD. An unrecorded version, or a recorded version
#              whose live arms differ, is reported UNREVIEWED (exit 4) — a
#              prompt for `skills/nexus.cc-update` collision analysis, not a
#              failure. Nothing here can make an unreviewed bump a red without
#              also making every other operator's host a red.
#
#   DELEGATES  <argument>  <CLAUDE.md block>  <what breaks if it stops>
#              The argument MUST match some arm, i.e. must reach `command grep`.
#
#   REACHES    <argument>  <CLAUDE.md block>  <what breaks if it stops>
#              The argument MUST match NO arm, i.e. must reach the embedded
#              ugrep.
#
# DELEGATES/REACHES are EVALUATED against the live arm set — the arms are run as
# a shell `case`, not string-compared — so a reworded but equivalent arm set
# passes and a genuinely moved boundary fails.

ARMS	2.1.246	-*-filter*|-*-pager*|-*-view*|-*-format-open*|-*-config*|---*|-@*|-*-save-config*|-[Zz]*|-[!-]*[Zz]*|--null|--null-data

DELEGATES	---	DASH-PATTERN-OPTION	Mode 1 (`grep -vF '---'`) is LOUD only because a `---`-leading argument is handed to GNU grep. Under the embedded ugrep it is SILENT (rc 0-1, empty stderr) — Mode 1 BECOMES Mode 2. If this stops being delegated, that entry's "at least Mode 1 is loud" reading is false on this harness, which is the entry's own dangerous direction.
REACHES	^\+\+\+	GREP-BRE-DIALECT	The entry's LOUD half is that a bare `grep -c '^\+\+\+'` is rejected by ugrep (invalid syntax, rc 2) while `command grep` silently keeps 1 line of 4. If this pattern started being delegated, the bare call would become the silent wrong answer too and the entry's contrast would be gone.
REACHES	-c	GREP-BRE-DIALECT	`-c` must not itself trigger delegation, or every counting call in that entry measures GNU rather than the wrapper and the entry describes an instrument nobody is using.
REACHES	-vF	DASH-PATTERN-OPTION	The flags that accompany Mode 1 must not be what causes the delegation, or the entry's attribution of the split to the `---` ARGUMENT is wrong even where its outcome is right.
